 "answers_to_coordinator": {
  "Q1_is_top1_an_overshoot_of_the_weighting": "NO -- it was the ADMITTED SET, not the weight function. The sensitivity sweep is in weight_sensitivity.json: over the original distance-chosen admitted set, every weighting trades top-1 mass against fragment mass on ONE axis, because 4-6 of the 8 admitted endpoints WERE fragments. archive_rank top1 0.581/eff 8.7/sub10 0.106; admitted_rank 0.326/18.7/0.446; softmax at T=1x,5x,20x the observed scale 0.696/6.6/0.079, 0.516/9.2/0.109, 0.248/20.8/0.480. There is no setting that buys breadth without buying fragments. Fixing CENTRE selection removes the tradeoff instead of navigating it: the admitted set goes from 6/8 sub-10 fragments to 2/8, 4/8 to 0/8 and 6/8 to 0/8, and admitted scores go from [0.22,0.21,0.026,0.026,0.069,0.027,0.081,0.081] to [0.22,0.21,0.188,0.174,0.143,0.107,0.134,0.112] on celecoxib.",
  "Q2_effective_parents": "exp(entropy) of the parent distribution: 23.5->25.2, 22.7->19.9, 23.2->12.0. The intermediate state the coordinator saw (evidence weighting over distance-chosen centres) was 8.7/7.6/7.8, which WAS too narrow. Top-1 now takes 4.3/5.7/7.8 of 16 queries per round rather than 9.3/10.9/10.1.",
  "Q3_stall": "IT DID LATCH, and it is now fixed. Measured on the frozen replay, 64 consecutive unproductive children moved the top parent's mass by 0.05%/0.27%/0.23% -- the allocator could not distinguish a working parent from a stalled one, because the tilt added reward-scale terms (~0.007) to a dimensionless base of 1.0. Rewritten as a ratio in measured-reward units (upside/observed_scale), the same probe now gives 16.7%/26.1%/32.6% decay, monotone in the number of dead children. No new constant: the divisor is the run's own counted mean upside per child."
 },
